> > > > On Friday, January 17, 2020 at 7:12:13 PM UTC-5, Ross Couvillon wrote:
> > > > > So to clarify using your example, could you segment summer range and winter range behavior and then arrive at an "annual home range" by either (1) simultaneously calculating summer and winter ranges as in the overlap example and then average them with the mean() function or (2) calculating summer and winter ranges individually going through the typical model selection process and adding them together?
> > > > > For Scenario 2 let's consider summer and winter ranges discrete areas (as in the individual I provided as an example) and winter home range is 2 ha and summer home range is 5 ha, so you get an "annual home range" of 7 ha divided between two UDs.
> > > > >
> > > > > Thanks again,
